<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Husni al-Za'im</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Husni al-Za'im was a Syrian army officer who led the first military coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>'s history in March 1949, overthrowing President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shukri-al-quwatli">Shukri al-Quwatli</a> after the defeat in the 1948 war with Israel. He made himself president, signed an armistice with Israel and introduced some reforms, but alienated his allies and was overthrown and executed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-al-hinnawi">Sami al-Hinnawi</a> in August 1949.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/husni-al-zaim.jpg" alt="Husni al-Za'im" width="252" height="450" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Husni_al-Zaiim.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Husni al-Za'im was born in 1897 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, then part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. His father served as a religious authority in the Ottoman army, and his mother was of Kurdish origin. He was educated in Aleppo and then at a military school in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he served in the Ottoman army and was captured by British forces; after his release he joined the army of Emir Faisal in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> instituted its mandate over Syria he became an officer in the French Army, and later he served in the army of the newly independent Syrian Republic. After the early defeats of Abdullah Atfeh in the 1948 Arab-Israeli War, he was made Chief of Staff.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The defeat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a> forces in that war shook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and undermined confidence in its unstable <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliamentary-democracy">parliamentary democracy</a>. On 30 March 1949 al-Za'im seized power in a bloodless coup, the first military coup in the history of Syria. Allegations that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-intelligence-agency">Central Intelligence Agency</a> engineered it are described as highly controversial; most of the available evidence suggests the decision was al-Za'im's alone, though he benefited from some American help in planning. Four days after the coup the Syrian government ratified the controversial Trans-Arabian Pipeline deal. President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shukri-al-quwatli">Shukri al-Quwatli</a> was briefly imprisoned and then released into exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and al-Za'im imprisoned many political leaders, among them Munir al-Ajlani.</p><p>His secular policies included support for votes for women, and he suggested they give up the veil, which stirred religious leaders. He also worked to abolish the fez. Higher taxes aggrieved businessmen, and Arab nationalists resented his cease-fire with Israel and his deals with American oil companies. He made a peace overture to Israel, offering to settle 300,000 Palestinian refugees in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> in exchange for border changes and half of Lake Tiberias, conditional on outside aid for the Syrian economy; it was answered slowly and not taken seriously.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lacking popular support, al-Za'im was overthrown after about four and a half months by his colleagues Adib al-Shishakli and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sami-al-hinnawi">Sami al-Hinnawi</a>. Hinnawi staged the coup on 13 August 1949, and al-Za'im was tried and executed by firing squad on 14 August, together with Prime Minister Muhsin al-Barazi. His takeover shattered the country's fragile democratic rule and set off a series of increasingly violent military revolts, and two more coups followed in August and December 1949. He had married Nouran in 1947, and she was first lady of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from April to August 1949.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Husni al-Za'im” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Independence brought coups by <b>Husni al-Za'im</b>, Sami al-Hinnawi and Adib Shishakli in 1949, a return to parliament in 1954, a pact with the Soviet Union in 1956 and, on 1 February 1958, union with Egypt as the United Arab Republic.</blockquote><a href="/empire/syrian-arab-republic">Syrian Arab Republic · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Defeat in the 1948 war with Israel discredited the civilian leadership, and in 1949 three coups followed, by <b>Husni al-Za'im</b>, Sami al-Hinnawi and Adib Shishakli, who ruled until 1954.</blockquote><a href="/state/syrian-republic">Syrian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.2,37.16&amp;year=1930" aria-label="Aleppo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Husni al-Za'im"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5493 4455h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5493 4455h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.2,37.16&amp;year=1930">What was here in 1930? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
